     SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

     Every value here was read off the running stock ROM, from
     work/device_dump/display/dumpsys-display.txt, rather than copied from
     another device. The relevant line is the DisplayDeviceInfo for
     "Built-in Screen".

     Nothing panel specific belongs in this file. The panel is dual sourced
     (BOE nt36536e / CSOT nt36536) and is selected by the bootloader on the
     kernel command line, resolved inside the prebuilt dtbo. Both suppliers run
     the same mode list, so these values hold for either one.
-->
<resources>
    <!-- The panel runs 30/60/90/120/144.

         ⚠️ config_defaultRefreshRate was 60 here, on the reasoning that 60 is
         "the rate the display settles to when nothing is driving it". That
         reasoning was inferred from an idle renderFrameRate reading rather than
         read off the resource, and it is wrong. DisplayModeDirector on the
         running stock ROM reports

             mDefaultRefreshRate: 144.0
             mDefaultPeakRefreshRate: 240.0

         mDefaultRefreshRate is R.integer.config_defaultRefreshRate, so stock
         sets it to 144 (via FrameworksResTarget_Vendor.apk, the vendor RRO this
         port replaces). At 60 it posts a PRIORITY_DEFAULT_RENDER_FRAME_RATE
         vote (DisplayModeDirector.java:1225-1227) that caps the render frame
         rate globally at 60 — on a 144 Hz panel.

         config_defaultPeakRefreshRate stays 144: it is a ceiling, and the panel
         cannot exceed it anyway, so 144 and stock's 240 are equivalent here. -->
    <integer name="config_defaultPeakRefreshRate">144</integer>
    <integer name="config_defaultRefreshRate">144</integer>

    <!-- Rounded display corners. Stock gets this from the same vendor RRO as
         config_defaultRefreshRate above; ro.sf.rounded_corner_radius is unset on
         this device, so without an override here SystemUI would not mask the
         corners at all.

         Keeping the unit as px is right: the corner radius is a property of the
         glass, so it must not move when TARGET_SCREEN_DENSITY does.

         The value, though, has been wrong twice, in opposite directions, and
         both times because the dp -> px conversion was anchored to the wrong
         density. Writing the derivation down so it stops happening:

             stock RRO declares    20dp
             stock default density 360   ->  20 * 360/160 = 45 px
             dump-time density     306   ->  20 * 306/160 = 38.25 -> 38 px

         An earlier revision said 45px and cited a dumpsys measurement; that was
         the right number with the wrong evidence. It was then "corrected" to
         38px because work/device_dump/display/dumpsys-display.txt:71 does report
             RoundedCorner{position=TopLeft, radius=38, center=Point(38, 38)}
         and wm.txt records "Override density: 306".

         But 306 was not a stock default and not a menu option misread as a
         value: the owner had set it by hand in Settings > Display size before
         that dump was taken (confirmed by the owner). It says nothing about the
         hardware. Stock's own default is 360: wm.txt:2 says "Physical density:
         360", and dumpsys-display.txt:201 mBaseDisplayInfo density 360 versus
         :202 mOverrideDisplayInfo density 306. So the radius Lenovo dimensioned
         the glass against is 45 px, and 38 was an artefact of one user setting.

         Confirmed directly rather than inferred: aapt2 dump of the stock vendor
         RRO reports
             work/unpacked/parts/vendor/overlay/FrameworksResTarget_Vendor.apk
             -> dimen/rounded_corner_radius  20dp
         and stock's own framework-res.apk leaves it at 0dp, so the RRO is where
         it comes from.

         TARGET_SCREEN_DENSITY is now 360, the same as stock, so 45px and 20dp
         are the same number here. Keep the unit as px anyway: the radius is a
         property of the glass and must not move if the density is ever
         retuned. That is the whole point of expressing it in px.

         ⚠️ Not 156px, which is what work/reference/hirero-malbec-device puts
         here. At 320 real ppi that would be 12.4 mm of corner masking on a
         panel whose vendor RRO says 3.6 mm. -->
    <dimen name="rounded_corner_radius">45px</dimen>

    <!-- ★ 0.0 here was a black screen waiting to happen.
         DisplayDeviceConfig.java:2057-2071 reads this straight into
         mBacklightMinimum whenever it is not the -2 sentinel, and that is the
         floor below which the brightness slider cannot go. At 0.0 the slider
         bottoms out at backlight 0 on panel0-backlight, i.e. the panel is off.
         The comment above it ("brightnessMinimum 0.0") had copied stock's
         DisplayDeviceInfo.brightnessMinimum, which is in *brightness* space, not
         the resource's space.
         Correct value = BrightnessSynchronizer.brightnessIntToFloat(2)
                       = (2 - 1) / (255 - 1) = 1/254 = 0.003937008,
         derived from stock's config_screenBrightnessSettingMinimum=2 and
         confirmed against the running device: dumpsys power reports
         mScreenBrightnessMinimum=0.003937008.
         brightnessMaximum 1.0 / brightnessDefault 0.39763778 are unchanged. -->
    <item name="config_screenBrightnessSettingMinimumFloat" format="float" type="dimen">0.003937008</item>
    <item name="config_screenBrightnessSettingMaximumFloat" format="float" type="dimen">1.0</item>
    <item name="config_screenBrightnessSettingDefaultFloat" format="float" type="dimen">0.39763778</item>

    <!-- ⚠️ There used to be a config_screenBrightnessDozeFloat of 0.05 here.
         That was a misread of the dump: 0.05 is the *dim* value, not the doze
         value. Checked again on the running device:
             mScreenBrightnessDozeConfig=0.0
             mScreenBrightnessDimConfig=0.05
         Both already match AOSP's defaults (frameworks/base/core/res/res/
         values/config.xml: doze 0.0, dim 0.05), so neither needs an override
         and writing the dim value into the doze resource only made AOD wrong. -->

    <!-- ★ config_mainDisplayShape is deliberately NOT carried over from the
         stock vendor RRO, even though that RRO does set it. Decoded, stock's
         value is

             M20 0 h1040 s20 0 20 20 v2360 s0 20 -20 20 h-1040 s-20 0 -20 -20 v-2360 ...

         i.e. a rounded rectangle 20+1040+20 = 1080 wide and 20+2360+20 = 2400
         tall. This panel is 2190x3504. The path is for some other model that
         shares Lenovo's vendor image — the same pattern as the
         vendor.lenovo.hardware.vibrator_dual-service entry in stock's
         file_contexts, whose binary does not exist on this device either.

         Copying it would tell every app calling WindowInsets#getDisplayShape()
         that the screen is 1080x2400. AOSP's default is the empty string, which
         makes DisplayShape.fromResources() synthesise the shape from the real
         display size and the rounded_corner_radius above — correct here, and
         correct on any future panel. Leaving it unset is the right answer, not
         an omission. -->

    <!-- No config_mainBuiltInDisplayCutout override: the panel has no cutout and
         AOSP's default for that resource is already the empty string
         (frameworks/base/core/res/res/values/config.xml). Restating it added
         nothing, and this file's stated policy is to record measured
         differences, not to copy framework-res. -->

    <!-- The device has no notification LED; that is the AOSP default already,
         so it is not restated here. Add entries only when the stock dump
         disagrees with AOSP, so this file stays a record of measured
         differences rather than a copy of framework-res. -->

    <!-- ⚠️ This block used to also claim that "no physical navigation keys" was
         an AOSP default needing no entry. That reasoning was backwards and
         would have shipped an unnavigable device.

         AOSP's default is false (frameworks/base/core/res/res/values/config.xml
         :2771), and DisplayPolicy.java:677-688 reads it to decide whether
         SystemUI creates a NavigationBar at all. false means no Home/Back/
         Recents AND no edge-back gesture handler.

         The device really does have no navigation keys — `getevent -pl` finds
         only gpio-keys VOLUMEUP, pmic_resin VOLUMEDOWN and pmic_pwrkey POWER —
         which is precisely why the bar has to be turned ON.

         Stock sets it true from product/overlay/LgsiFrameworksOverlay.apk, and
         PixelOS replaces the whole product partition, so that RRO is gone.
         Without this the device boots to Launcher and cannot be operated
         without the folio keyboard. onyx's overlay sets it too. -->
    <bool name="config_showNavigationBar">true</bool>

    <!-- Wired headset detect goes through input events, not the audio HAL.
         AOSP default is false (config.xml:3464); stock sets true in
         vendor/overlay/FrameworksResTarget_Vendor.apk, which we also drop.
         Confirmed on the device: event5 "tuna-qrd-snd-card Headset Jack"
         reports SW_HEADPHONE_INSERT / SW_MICROPHONE_INSERT / SW_LINEOUT_INSERT
         and event6 "Button Jack" reports KEY_MEDIA / KEY_VOLUME{UP,DOWN}.
         Without this, plug/unplug and inline buttons do nothing. -->
    <bool name="config_useDevInputEventForAudioJack">true</bool>

    <!-- The QTI power stack expects auto-suspend to be decoupled from the
         display. AOSP default is false (config.xml:3027); stock sets true, and
         the running device confirms it: `dumpsys power` shows
         mDecoupleHalAutoSuspendModeFromDisplayConfig=true and
         mHalAutoSuspendModeEnabled=true. onyx sets it as well. -->
    <bool name="config_powerDecoupleAutoSuspendModeFromDisplay">true</bool>
    <!-- ================================================================== -->
    <!-- NEW: adaptive brightness                                           -->
    <!-- ================================================================== -->

    <!-- Flag indicating whether we should enable the automatic brightness.
         Software implementation will be used if config_hardware_auto_brightness_available is not set -->
    <bool name="config_automatic_brightness_available">true</bool>

    <!-- Array of light sensor lux values to define our levels for auto backlight brightness support.
         The N entries of this array define N + 1 control points as follows:
         (1-based arrays)

         Point 1:            (0, value[1]):             lux <= 0
         Point 2:     (level[1], value[2]):  0        < lux <= level[1]
         Point 3:     (level[2], value[3]):  level[2] < lux <= level[3]
         ...
         Point N+1: (level[N], value[N+1]):  level[N] < lux

         The control points must be strictly increasing.  Each control point
         corresponds to an entry in the brightness backlight values arrays.
         For example, if lux == level[1] (first element of the levels array)
         then the brightness will be determined by value[2] (second element
         of the brightness values array).

         Spline interpolation is used to determine the auto-brightness
         backlight values for lux levels between these control points.

         Must be overridden in platform specific overlays -->
    <integer-array name="config_autoBrightnessLevels">
        <item>5</item>
        <item>15</item>
        <item>40</item>
        <item>100</item>
        <item>180</item>
        <item>400</item>
        <item>550</item>
        <item>1000</item>
        <item>1500</item>
        <item>2000</item>
        <item>2500</item>
        <item>3000</item>
        <item>4000</item>
        <item>5000</item>
    </integer-array>

    <!-- Array of desired screen brightness in nits corresponding to the lux values
         in the config_autoBrightnessLevels array. The display brightness is defined as the measured
         brightness of an all-white image.

         If this is defined then:
            - config_autoBrightnessLcdBacklightValues should not be defined
            - config_screenBrightnessNits must be defined
            - config_screenBrightnessBacklight must be defined

         This array should have size one greater than the size of the config_autoBrightnessLevels
         array. The brightness values must be non-negative and non-decreasing. This must be
         overridden in platform specific overlays -->
    <array name="config_autoBrightnessDisplayValuesNits">
        <item>8</item>
        <item>8</item>
        <item>30</item>
        <item>70</item>
        <item>100</item>
        <item>120</item>
        <item>150</item>
        <item>180</item>
        <item>210</item>
        <item>270</item>
        <item>340</item>
        <item>420</item>
        <item>480</item>
        <item>550</item>
        <item>650</item>
    </array>

    <!-- An array describing the screen's backlight values corresponding to the brightness
         values in the config_screenBrightnessNits array.

         This array should be equal in size to config_screenBrightnessBacklight. -->
    <integer-array name="config_screenBrightnessBacklight">
        <item>0</item>
        <item>4</item>
        <item>13</item>
        <item>30</item>
        <item>43</item>
        <item>51</item>
        <item>64</item>
        <item>77</item>
        <item>90</item>
        <item>115</item>
        <item>145</item>
        <item>179</item>
        <item>205</item>
        <item>235</item>
        <item>255</item>
    </integer-array>

    <!-- An array of floats describing the screen brightness in nits corresponding to the backlight
         values in the config_screenBrightnessBacklight array.  On OLED displays these  values
         should be measured with an all white image while the display is in the fully on state.
         Note that this value should *not* reflect the maximum brightness value for any high
         brightness modes but only the maximum brightness value obtainable in a sustainable manner.

         This array should be equal in size to config_screenBrightnessBacklight -->
    <array name="config_screenBrightnessNits">
        <item>0</item>
        <item>8</item>
        <item>30</item>
        <item>70</item>
        <item>100</item>
        <item>120</item>
        <item>150</item>
        <item>180</item>
        <item>210</item>
        <item>270</item>
        <item>340</item>
        <item>420</item>
        <item>480</item>
        <item>550</item>
        <item>650</item>
    </array>

    <!-- Stability requirements in milliseconds for accepting a new brightness level.  This is used
         for debouncing the light sensor.  Different constants are used to debounce the light sensor
         when adapting to brighter or darker environments.  This parameter controls how quickly
         brightness changes occur in response to an observed change in light level that exceeds the
         hysteresis threshold. -->
    <integer name="config_autoBrightnessBrighteningLightDebounce">2000</integer>
    <integer name="config_autoBrightnessDarkeningLightDebounce">3000</integer>

    <!-- Note: This setting is deprecated, please use
    config_screenBrightnessSettingMinimumFloat instead -->
    <!-- Kept in sync with the float above (2 -> 1/254). PowerManager.java:1380
         exposes this int directly via getMinimumScreenBrightnessSetting(), and it
         is the fallback DisplayDeviceConfig uses if the float ever reverts to -2. -->
    <integer name="config_screenBrightnessSettingMinimum">2</integer>

    <!-- Screen brightness used to dim the screen while dozing in a very low power state.
         May be less than the minimum allowed brightness setting
         that can be set by the user. -->
    <!-- Set the int only, not config_screenBrightnessDozeFloat: AOSP's float default
         is 0.0 rather than the -2 sentinel, so DisplayDeviceConfig never falls back
         to this int. It is SystemUI that consumes it, at
         DozeParameters.java:209 (value / 255f). Stock uses 17. -->
    <integer name="config_screenBrightnessDoze">17</integer>

    <!-- ================================================================== -->
    <!-- Folio cover: close to sleep, open to wake                          -->
    <!-- ================================================================== -->

    <!-- Paired with configs/device_state_configuration.xml, which defines
         state 0 = OPENED and state 1 = CLOSED from the BU52053NVX hall sensor.
         That file carries the evidence for the mapping and the polarity.

         Both these arrays AND the PROPERTY_POWER_CONFIGURATION_TRIGGER_*
         properties in the XML are required, because LogicalDisplayMapper
         branches on a feature flag and reads a different one on each side:
         shouldDeviceBeWoken / shouldDeviceBePutToSleep (:642-700) use the
         properties when mDeviceStateManagerFlags.deviceStatePropertyMigration()
         is on and these arrays when it is not. Setting only one half works
         until the flag flips.

         AOSP's defaults are both empty (core/res/res/values/config.xml:731,740),
         which is why the folio does nothing today. -->
    <integer-array name="config_deviceStatesOnWhichToWakeUp">
        <item>0</item>
    </integer-array>
    <integer-array name="config_deviceStatesOnWhichToSleep">
        <item>1</item>
    </integer-array>

    <!-- ================================================================== -->
    <!-- NEW: PinnerService                                                 -->
    <!-- ================================================================== -->

    <!-- Default files to pin via Pinner Service -->
    <!-- Stock's list has 9 entries. Two of them, /system/framework/arm64/
         boot-QPerformance.oat and boot-UxPerformance.oat, are QTI perf JARs that
         PixelOS does not build (verified absent from out/target/product/malbec),
         so they are dropped. The remaining 7 were each confirmed present in the
         build tree. -->
    <string-array translatable="false" name="config_defaultPinnerServiceFiles">
        <item>/system/framework/framework.jar</item>
        <item>/system/framework/services.jar</item>
        <item>/apex/com.android.media/javalib/updatable-media.jar</item>
        <item>/system/lib64/libhwui.so</item>
        <item>/vendor/lib64/libllvm-qgl.so</item>
        <item>/vendor/lib64/libllvm-glnext.so</item>
        <item>/vendor/lib64/libsdmextension.so</item>
    </string-array>

    <!-- ================================================================== -->
    <!-- NEW: power behaviour (stock parity, optional)                      -->
    <!-- ================================================================== -->

    <!-- If this is true, the screen will come on when you unplug usb/power/whatever. -->
    <bool name="config_unplugTurnsOnScreen">true</bool>

    <!-- ================================================================== -->
    <!-- Doze / Always-On Display                                           -->
    <!-- ================================================================== -->

    <!-- Without this the entire doze subsystem is inert, and the omission is
         silent. AOSP's default is the empty string
         (frameworks/base/core/res/res/values/config.xml:2932) and nothing in
         vendor/, device/ or packages/ sets it. The chain:
         AmbientDisplayConfiguration.ambientDisplayAvailable() is
         !isEmpty(config_dozeComponent) -> false, so
         pulseOnNotificationAvailable() is false and every doze feature is
         disabled; DreamManagerService then gets a null ComponentName and
         DozeService is never bound.

         This tree was already self-inconsistent about it: config_screenBrightnessDoze
         above cites DozeParameters.java, which is code inside DozeService,
         a service that never ran. Both stock RROs decode to exactly this value,
         and so do both peer trees. -->
    <string name="config_dozeComponent" translatable="false">com.android.systemui/com.android.systemui.doze.DozeService</string>

    <!-- ================================================================== -->
    <!-- Colour modes                                                       -->
    <!-- ================================================================== -->

    <!-- AOSP's default is an EMPTY array (core/res/res/values/config.xml:1079-1085),
         which makes ColorDisplayService.isColorModeAvailable() always false and
         leaves colorMode at NOT_SET — so Settings > Display > Colors vanishes
         entirely. So an override is needed; the question is which values.

         The four AOSP modes, and only those:
             0 NATURAL   1 BOOSTED   2 SATURATED   3 AUTOMATIC (shown as "Adaptive")

         ⚠️ This used to also list the ten vendor-range modes 256-265
         (VENDOR_COLOR_MODE_RANGE_MIN = 256), copied from stock. The owner
         reported the symptom precisely: those entries appear in Settings with
         NO NAME, and selecting one does nothing. Both halves are explained, and
         neither is fixable from a device overlay:

           - No name. Settings takes the list of modes from
             com.android.internal.R.array.config_availableColorModes
             (ColorModeUtils.kt:66) but takes the NAMES from its own
             Settings/res/values/config.xml:625-640, which defines exactly four
             values: 0,1,2,3. getColorModeMapping() returns a map built from
             those, so colorModesToSummaries.get(256) is null and
             ColorModePreferenceFragment.getCandidates() adds a candidate with a
             null title.
           - No effect. DisplayTransformManager.java:282-286 passes a vendor
             mode straight through to setDisplayColor(colorMode), which is
             SurfaceFlinger binder transaction 1023, and
             SurfaceFlinger.cpp:7255-7256 does
                 mDisplayColorSetting = static_cast<DisplayColorSetting>(value)
             DisplayColorSetting only has MANAGED/UNMANAGED/ENHANCED, so 256-265
             cast to an out-of-range value that no branch matches. These modes
             exist only in QTI's SurfaceFlinger fork
             (libsurfaceflingerextension), and PixelOS builds AOSP's.
             Independently confirmed on the running device: SurfaceFlinger
             reports exactly three supported colour modes — NATIVE, SRGB,
             DISPLAY_P3 — with no vendor entries at all.

         2 (SATURATED) is ADDED, having been missing before. It is the one mode
         with a guaranteed visible effect: DisplayTransformManager maps it to
         DISPLAY_COLOR_UNMANAGED, i.e. render straight to the panel's native
         gamut with no colour management. -->
    <integer-array name="config_availableColorModes">
        <item>0</item>
        <item>1</item>
        <item>2</item>
        <item>3</item>
    </integer-array>

    <!-- Three values the factory ROM sets through RROs on the *product*
         partition, which PixelOS replaces. Nothing warns when they disappear:
         each one silently falls back to the AOSP default and the feature just
         behaves differently. Recovered by dumping every stock RRO that targets
         `android` with aapt2 and diffing against the built framework-res.apk
         (which already includes the PixelOS/LineageOS static overlays). -->

    <!-- Stock: true, from product/overlay/LgsiFrameworksOverlay.apk.
         AOSP default is false (core/res/res/values/config.xml). Consumer is
         SystemUI KeyguardStatusBarRepository.kt:71 — with it false there is no
         user switcher on the lock screen, which matters on a tablet where
         multi-user is a headline feature. -->
    <bool name="config_keyguardUserSwitcher">true</bool>

    <!-- Stock: true, from product/overlay/FrameworksResCommon_Sys.apk.
         AudioManager.java:7054 turns this into
         PROPERTY_SUPPORT_AUDIO_SOURCE_UNPROCESSED. The QTI HAL does support the
         unprocessed source, so reporting the AOSP default of false tells
         recording apps a capability the hardware has is unavailable. -->
    <bool name="config_supportAudioSourceUnprocessed">true</bool>

    <!-- Stock: 2500 / 4628 / 3420 (LgsiFrameworksOverlay). AOSP defaults are
         2596 / 4082 / 2850. These are Night Light's colour-temperature range and
         default; the stock triple is calibrated for this panel. Night Light is
         genuinely reachable here — config_setColorTransformAccelerated is
         already true via vendor/lineage/overlay/common/frameworks/base/core/res/
         res/values/config.xml:154 — so these are live, not dead keys. -->
    <integer name="config_nightDisplayColorTemperatureMin">2500</integer>
    <integer name="config_nightDisplayColorTemperatureMax">4628</integer>
    <integer name="config_nightDisplayColorTemperatureDefault">3420</integer>

</resources>
